"10 euros.","4 euros."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c0257597e66bff2dd3adf7e0","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_stationary_bonus_malus_premium_96029ff6_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A two-class chain deterministically switches class each year. Its transition rows are (0,1),(1,0), and prices are 100,200 euros. What disproves convergence from every initial law despite its unique stationary law?","options":["Starting in class 1 gives the stationary premium 150 every year.","The vector(1/2,1/2) fails the balance equation.","Starting in class 1 gives alternating laws and premiums 100,200.","The two concentrated class laws are both stationary."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"34b1d30803ccde82fcbde997","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_stationary_bonus_malus_premium_96029ff6_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A three-class chain never changes class: P is the identity. Prices are 100,150,200 euros. Does a stationary mean premium 150 identify a unique stationary distribution?","options":["Yes; only the middle class can contribute to a mean 150.","No; both(0,1,0) and(1/2,0,1/2) are stationary with that mean.","Yes; normalisation makes an identity-chain law unique.","No; the identity matrix is inadmissible because stationarity requires a unique law."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e42cfa5a35783cdca42b495a","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}